.TH AGENT 1
.SH NAME
agent \- interactive LLM coding assistant
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B agent
[
.I conn
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Agent
is an interactive coding assistant that drives a large language model
through a filesystem interface and exposes a small set of tools for
reading, editing, and exploring the local system.
.PP
The model is reached through a 9P service mounted at
.BR /n/llm .
If
.I conn
is supplied it names an existing session (a numeric id under
.BR /n/llm );
otherwise
.I agent
opens
.B /n/llm/clone
to acquire a fresh connection and uses that for the duration of the
session.
Before starting,
.I agent
reads
.B /n/llm/info
to discover the model's context window and caps its own history accordingly.
.PP
Each iteration reads a line from standard input, appends it to the
conversation history, and writes the full prompt (system prompt plus
turn-marked history) to
.BI /n/llm/ conn /data \fR.
It then reads the model's reply from the same file, streaming
non-tool text to standard output.
If the reply contains a tool call, it is parsed, dispatched, and the
result is fed back to the model as another turn; the loop continues
until the model produces a reply with no tool call.
.PP
The system prompt is read from
.B /lib/llm/system.md
if present, otherwise from
.B ./system.md
in the working directory, with a built-in fallback if neither exists.
The list of available tools is appended from
.B /lib/llm/tools.md
or
.BR ./tools.md .
.SS Tool calls
To invoke a tool the model emits a JSON block wrapped in exactly
.B ```json
and
.BR ``` ,
with fields
.B name
and
.BR arguments .
The alternative framings
.B <tool_call>\fI...\fB</tool_call>
and
.B ```tool_call
are also recognised.
For example:
.IP
.EX
```json
{
  "name": "read",
  "arguments": {
    "path": "agent.b",
    "offset": 100,
    "limit": 40
  }
}
```
.EE
.SS Input
Input lines are treated as user turns.
Two prefixes are special:
.TP 10
.B !
The remainder of the line is passed to
.IR sh (1)
and runs locally without touching the model.
.TP
.B exit
.PD 0
.TP
.B quit
Terminate the session.
.PD
.SH TOOLS
.TP
.B read
Read a file.
Arguments:
.I path
(required),
.I offset
(1-indexed line, default 1),
.I limit
(default 2000).
.TP
.B write
Create or overwrite a file, creating parent directories as needed.
Arguments:
.I path
and
.IR content .
.TP
.B edit
Replace an exact, unique occurrence of
.I oldText
with
.IR newText .
The edit is refused if
.I oldText
is not found or matches more than once.
Arguments:
.IR path ,
.IR oldText ,
.IR newText .
.TP
.B multi_edit
Apply an ordered list of
.B edit
operations to one file atomically.
Each step sees the output of prior steps; if any step fails, the file
is left untouched.
Arguments:
.I path
and
.I edits
(a JSON array of
.BI { oldText , newText }
objects).
.TP
.B shell
Run
.I command
under
.IR sh (1)
with standard output and standard error captured together.
Optional
.I timeout
in seconds; if the command does not finish in time it is abandoned and
an error is returned.
.TP
.B ls
List a directory, or stat a single file.
Each entry is printed as
.BI "kind length name\fR,
with
.B d
for directories and
.B -
for regular files.
Argument:
.IR path .
.TP
.B glob
Walk
.I dir
recursively and return basenames matching
.IR pattern .
The pattern accepts a single
.B *
wildcard as a prefix or suffix (for example
.B *.b
or
.BR test_* ).
Results are capped at 500 entries.
.TP
.B grep
Search for
.I pattern
(a regular expression, see
.IR regex (2))
in
.IR path ,
which may be a shell glob such as
.BR /appl/cmd/*.b .
Internally dispatches to
.IR grep (1).
.SH EXAMPLES
Start against a freshly cloned session:
.IP
.EX
agent
.EE
.PP
Attach to session
.B 3
already present under
.BR /n/llm :
.IP
.EX
agent 3
.EE
.PP
Run a local shell command without consulting the model:
.IP
.EX
> ! ls -l /appl/cmd
.EE
.SH FILES
.TF /n/llm/\fIconn\fB/data
.TP
.B /n/llm/clone
Read to acquire a new session id.
.TP
.BI /n/llm/ conn /ctl
Control file; the string
.B reset
is written before each prompt to clear the server's context.
.TP
.BI /n/llm/ conn /data
Write prompt, read reply.
.TP
.B /n/llm/info
Model metadata; parsed for
.BR context_length .
.TP
.B /lib/llm/system.md
.PD 0
.TP
.B ./system.md
.PD
System prompt, first match wins.
.TP
.B /lib/llm/tools.md
.PD 0
.TP
.B ./tools.md
.PD
Tool documentation appended to the system prompt.
.TP
.B ./transcript.log
Every outgoing prompt is appended here, framed by a banner.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/agent.b
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.IR sh (1),
.IR grep (1),
.IR regex (2)
.SH BUGS
A
.B shell
call that exceeds its
.I timeout
is abandoned, not killed; the subprocess continues to run until it
exits of its own accord.
.PP
The
.B glob
pattern syntax is a narrow subset of
.IR sh (1)
globbing: one
.B *
only, as a prefix or suffix of the basename.
.PP
Context-window accounting estimates one token per three characters and
drops the oldest history entry when the estimate exceeds the reported
.BR context_length ;
it does not attempt real tokenisation.
